Reading, and an AMENDMENT of the main design (made after the pilot, whose purpose was to set the
grid, and before any main run):
 - Per input the kernel's survival is nearly a step in the rate: in 11 of 12 inputs the fraction
   alive goes from 100% (6 of 6) to 0–33% within one pilot step of 0.15 in log k. A main grid with
   steps of 0.08 would then locate k50 only to about ±0.04 per arm, i.e. e_ω to about ±0.15, however
   many runs per rate. So the main grid is changed to 21 rates = pilot k50 × exp(−0.24:0.024:0.24)
   with 10 runs per rate and arm (210 runs per arm instead of 240; same estimator, same seeds rule).
   The pilot locates k50 within about ±0.08, and arm shifts of up to ±0.12 (e = −0.6) stay inside.
 - Level of the table model A against the kernel (pilot k50 / model A's k50): 0.82–1.28 in 11
   inputs, 0.51 for input 10 (h² 0.74, m 0.0015, L 1000; its own table row died at 542 at a rate
   0.76 of the model's k50, so the model, linear in the logs of the other inputs, is off there, not
   the kernel).

